# WeBWorK problem written by Alex Jordan 
# Portland Community College
# ENDDESCRIPTION




##############################################

DOCUMENT();

loadMacros(
  "PGstandard.pl",
  "MathObjects.pl",
  "parserNumberWithUnits.pl",
  "PGML.pl",
);

##############################################
TEXT(beginproblem());


Context("Numeric");

$windSpeed = random(10,30,0.1);
$w = $windSpeed;
$airSpeed = random(180,300,1);

$distance[0] = random(600,1000,10);
$d0 = $distance[0];
$distance[1] = ($airSpeed-$windSpeed)*$distance[0]/($airSpeed+$windSpeed);
$distance[1] = round($distance[1]);
$d1 = $distance[1];

$airSpeed = -$w*($d0+$d1)/($d1-$d0);

$windSpeed = NumberWithUnits("$windSpeed","mi/hr");
$airSpeed = NumberWithUnits("$airSpeed","mi/hr");
$distance[0] = NumberWithUnits("$distance[0]","mi");
$distance[1] = NumberWithUnits("$distance[1]","mi");



##############################################

Context()->texStrings;

BEGIN_TEXT


When there is a \($windSpeed\) wind, an airplane can fly \($distance[0]\) with the wind in the same time that it can fly \($distance[1]\) against the wind. Find the speed of the plane when there is no wind.
$PAR

The plane's airspeed is \{ans_rule(15)\} when there is no wind.



END_TEXT

Context()->normalStrings;

##############################################
$showPartialCorrectAnswers = 1;

ANS( $airSpeed->cmp(tolerance=>0.01));

##############################################

$a = $airSpeed;
$com = Formula("(x+$w)*(x-$w)");
$w2 = $w**2;
$e = $d0*$w;
$f = $d1*$w;
$g = $d0-$d1;
$h = $f+$e;

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION

Assume the plane travels at [`x`] miles per hour when there is no wind. It's given that the wind speed is [`[$w]`] miles per hour. Then, the plane flies at [`x+[$w]`] miles per hour with the wind, and at [`x-[$w]`] miles per hour against the wind.

Using the formula [`\text{time}=\frac{\text{distance}}{\text{speed}}`], we know the plane can travel [`[$d0]`] miles with the wind in [`\frac{[$d0]}{x+[$w]}`] hours; and it can travel [`[$d1]`] miles against the wind in [`\frac{[$d1]}{x-[$w]}`] hours.

It's given that it takes the same time in both trips, so we can write an equation and solve it:

    [``
\begin{aligned}
  &\frac{[$d0]}{x+[$w]} = \frac{[$d1]}{x-[$w]} \\
  &\frac{[$d0]}{x+[$w]} \cdot [$com] = \frac{[$d1]}{x-[$w]} \cdot [$com] \\
  &[$d0](x-[$w]) = [$d1](x+[$w]) \\
  &[$d0]x-[$e] = [$d1]x+[$f] \\
  &[$g]x=[$h] \\
  &x \approx [$airSpeed]
\end{aligned}
    ``]

THe plane's airspeed is [`[$airSpeed]`] when there is no wind.

END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();
